## Interfaces as Type Filters

```xiom
fn print_hash[T: Hash](value: &T) {
  let h = value.hash();  // OK: T: Hash guarantees hash() exists
  io.println("hash: " + h.to_str());
}
```

Without `T: Hash`, calling `value.hash()` would be a compile error.

### Bounds Enable Method Access
```xiom
// Each bound unlocks methods:
fn demo[T: Clone + Display + Ord](value: &T) {
  let copy = value.clone();     // T: Clone
  io.println(copy.to_str());    // T: Display
  let cmp = value > &copy;      // T: Ord
}
```

### Bounds as Documentation
```xiom
// Without reading the body, you know what T needs:
fn store[T: Eq + Hash](value: T) { /* ... */ }
// T must support equality and hashing — probably a HashMap key

fn sort_items[T: Ord](items: &Vec[T]) { /* ... */ }
// T must support ordering — clearly sorting
```

### Composing Bounds
```xiom
// Multiple bounds on the same param:
fn complex[T: Clone + Display + Default + Eq](value: T) { /* ... */ }

// Different bounds on different params:
fn transform[I: Display, O: Display + Default](input: I) -> O { /* ... */ }
```

### The Error Message
When you try to use a type that doesn't satisfy a bound:
```
Error: type 'MyType' does not implement interface 'Ord'
  required by bound 'T: Ord' in 'sort_items'
```

